<Docs slug="adjust">
  <p>
    The panel on the right has three pages, picked from the icons on its
    edge &mdash; Adjustments, Crop &amp; Transform and Cleanup &mdash; and
    almost everything happens under <strong>Adjustments</strong>:
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>White Balance</strong> &mdash; Temp and Tint, or click
      <strong>Pick Gray</strong> and then click a neutral-gray pixel in the
      photo to balance from it. Fastest way out of a yellow room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tone</strong> &mdash; Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows,
      Whites and Blacks. This is where most photos are won or lost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Presence</strong> &mdash; Vibrance and Saturation. Vibrance
      leaves skin alone where it can, so try it before Saturation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detail</strong> &mdash; Denoise. <kbd>[</kbd> and <kbd>]</kbd>
      turn the photo in 90&deg; steps, and the <strong>Crop &amp;
      Transform</strong> page crops and straightens it. <kbd>C</kbd> starts a
      crop from the keyboard, <kbd>Enter</kbd> applies it and <kbd>Esc</kbd>
      cancels.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three habits that make it faster</h2>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Let the histogram start.</strong> <strong>Auto Adjust</strong> sets the
      tone sliders from the photo's own histogram &mdash;
      <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>U</kbd> for the photo in front of you,
      <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>U</kbd> for everything selected.
      <strong>Reset</strong> puts the sliders back to zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do one, then copy it.</strong>
      <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> copies the adjustment
      off a finished photo; select the rest of the shoot and press
      <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> to apply it to the whole
      selection. One good frame becomes the look for the shoot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Heal the small stuff.</strong> Touch Up, on the Cleanup page,
      removes spots &mdash; press <kbd>Q</kbd> to turn it on (<kbd>K</kbd>
      just opens the page), click a blemish, and size the brush with
      <kbd>[</kbd> / <kbd>]</kbd> or the Size slider, up to 64 spots
      per photo. It needs the full-resolution image loaded first, so give it a
      moment if you have just arrived from the grid.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of this touches the original file. Every slider is an instruction
    stored in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside your photo, so you
    can close the app mid-edit, come back next week, or reset the whole photo
    without any harm done. When it looks right, the last step is
    <a href="/docs/export.html">export</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
